// Chapter 7 - Evaluating Expressions
//
// Chapter 7's Lox is an expression evaluator: there are no statements yet, so
// a script is exactly ONE expression and the interpreter prints its value.
// That means this file is a single expression, and the lines below it are
// comments showing the other expressions worth trying.
//
// Run it:
//
//   ./scripts/lox.sh examples/ch07_evaluating.lox      ->  7
//   ./scripts/lox.sh --ast examples/ch07_evaluating.lox    (chapter 6's tree)
//   ./scripts/lox.sh --tokens examples/ch07_evaluating.lox (chapter 4's tokens)
//
// Or start the REPL and paste any line from the table below:
//
//   ./scripts/lox.sh

1 + 2 * 3

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Arithmetic and precedence                           prints
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   1 + 2 * 3                                         7
//   (1 + 2) * 3                                       9
//   10 / 4                                            2.5
//   10 / 5                                            2        (not "2.0")
//   -3 * -3                                           9
//   0.1 + 0.2                                         0.30000000000000004
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Strings
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   "cr" + "aft"                                      craft
//   "1" + "2"                                         12       (not 3)
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Truthiness: only false and nil are falsey (7.2.4)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   !true                                             false
//   !nil                                              true
//   !0                                                false    (0 is TRUTHY)
//   !""                                               false    ("" is TRUTHY)
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Comparison and equality (7.2.5)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   1 < 2                                             true
//   1 == "1"                                          false    (no coercion)
//   nil == false                                      false
//   (0 / 0) == (0 / 0)                                true     (see below)
//
// Lox numbers are doubles, so dividing by zero gives an IEEE 754 value
// rather than an error:
//
//   1 / 0                                             Infinity
//   0 / 0                                             NaN
//
// ...and yet NaN equals itself here, because the book's `isEqual` uses
// Java's `equals()` rather than `==`. The book calls this out: Lox
// deliberately does not follow IEEE on equality. Challenge 7.3 offers the
// other treatment of a zero divisor.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Runtime errors (7.3) - these print to stderr and exit 70
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   -"muffin"              Operand must be a number.
//   2 * (3 / -"muffin")    Operand must be a number.    (unwinds * and /)
//   1 + nil                Operands must be two numbers or two strings.
//   "a" < "b"              Operands must be numbers.    (challenge 7.1)
